  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nBreaks of sun have allowed temperatures to warm this afternoon \nwith dew points remaining in the upper 60s and low 70s. SPC \nmesoanalysis shows 1000 to 2000 Joules of Surface CAPE as of 2 \npm. Water vapor imagery does have dry air in place overhead so \nas it continues to warm, we may begin to dry out which will \nlower the SBCAPE. There is also not an evident trigger/shortwave\ncoming in to help spark off some storms until late this \nafternoon or evening which could be after we stabilize. Shear \nis still sufficient for strong storms and supercell potential if\nsomething was to break through the cap and develop over the \nnext few hours. There is some evidence that storms could \norganize into a squall line again in western PA and move into \nNEPA early tonight between 9 pm and 11 pm so that area continues \nto be highlighted by SPC with a sliver of a marginal risk and \nslight risk. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nAfter today, the ridge in the central US will flatten a bit \nallowing for the trough in New England to nudge in. This puts us\nunder cooler, north to northwest flow from tomorrow into early \nnext week. While it does look fairly dry, the cooler flow over \nthe warm Great Lakes may result in some lake effect rain showers\nat times though they will be spotty and short lived if they do \neven occur.",
